  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "react-intake-sme-01",
      "severity": "medium",
      "location": {
        "file": "standard-fast-track/react/effects-routing-and-rendering/loading-and-error-states/topic.json",
        "line": 29
      },
      "claim": "Objective o2 says fetch rejects only on network failure. The Fetch API can reject for other request failures, including a bad URL scheme (and cancellation can reject with AbortError); the accurate distinction is that HTTP error statuses such as 404 still fulfill with a Response, so response.ok must be checked.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API/Using_Fetch",
          "section": "Handling the response (fetch rejects on some errors such as network error or bad scheme, but fulfills for HTTP error statuses)",
          "retrievedAt": "2026-10-12T22:07:19Z",
          "textSha256": "dff01ff09ba232f3494bff39ae7e704cdd1d3068eeb1e439a0c3c5119f76c978"
        }
      ],
      "recommendation": "Keep the response.ok learning outcome, but replace 'because fetch rejects only on network failure' with 'because fetch does not reject merely because the server returned a non-2xx HTTP status' (or equivalent). Treat aborts and malformed requests separately when the page is authored.",
      "reviewedSha256": "1856808151d268b1052f228edb014e4ead80769a3dc613ba65e6309dca3ba1cb"
    },
    {
      "id": "react-intake-sme-02",
      "severity": "medium",
      "location": {
        "file": "standard-fast-track/react/auth-and-review/handling-401-and-403-redirect-and-sign-out/topic.json",
        "line": 25
      },
      "claim": "Objective o1 defines 401 as 'not authenticated' and 403 as 'authenticated but not allowed' without limiting that interpretation to the project API. HTTP defines 401 as lacking valid authentication credentials and 403 as understood but refused; 403 does not itself prove that the requester is authenticated. The intake's turning-off-the-dev-profile objective also correctly acknowledges that this back end may refuse an unauthenticated request with either status depending on configuration.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Status/401",
          "section": "Status (request lacks valid authentication credentials)",
          "retrievedAt": "2026-10-12T22:07:19Z",
          "textSha256": "293bf98c4cb1fca2900aa5a6d53b5fd6fbda7c1b65066b31325e658e59a2ee61"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html",
          "section": "15.5.4 403 Forbidden (server understood the request but refuses to fulfill it)",
          "retrievedAt": "2026-10-12T22:07:19Z",
          "textSha256": "d431760660ea44e130f6e919dab216df2d0b3a490567a98089267523368fe1e5"
        }
      ],
      "recommendation": "State the general protocol meanings first, then make the app-specific contract explicit: for this project API, 401 is the signal to clear client authentication state and start sign-in, while 403 means keep the session and show a not-authorized view. Avoid teaching authenticated status as an invariant of every 403 response, and distinguish clearing client state from expiring an HttpOnly session cookie on the server.",
      "reviewedSha256": "9bd13167fde80b5f8df8ec73789ac7cb2c7ede05b3821261c7305a7b2f299cc1"
    },
    {
      "id": "react-intake-sme-03",
      "severity": "medium",
      "location": {
        "file": "standard-fast-track/react/effects-routing-and-rendering/what-makes-a-component-re-render/topic.json",
        "line": 25
      },
      "claim": "Objective o1 presents own-state updates and parent renders as the complete list of re-render triggers. A component that reads context also re-renders when the provider supplies a changed value. Because this objective is included in the module-check blueprint, its exhaustive wording would make an incomplete rule both taught and assessed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "url": "https://react.dev/reference/react/useContext",
          "section": "Reference / Caveats (React automatically re-renders components that read changed context)",
          "retrievedAt": "2026-10-12T22:07:20Z",
          "textSha256": "e90f21b7ca9f6cf34e0f01891baa0cbd9cec5fae4e81aa750cf4474542fa79b5"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://react.dev/learn/render-and-commit",
          "section": "Step 1: Trigger a render; rendering nested components",
          "retrievedAt": "2026-10-12T22:07:20Z",
          "textSha256": "ce8d13879bb815e61f11e9c25c22de45505cb6f13e957ef4ff2aaff052f81e93"
        }
      ],
      "recommendation": "Without reordering or expanding the syllabus, make o1 non-exhaustive (for example, 'Identify state updates and parent renders as two causes taught so far') and explicitly connect changed context as another trigger in the later sharing-state-with-context topic and React review. Update the module-check expectation accordingly.",
      "reviewedSha256": "076a17bbaec6853dfcb848a6d2713884799e333e6e9bd380a75c205140bf4564"
    }
  ]
